  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cooler, broad cyclonic flow continues crossing the lower Great \nLakes early this morning. There is just enough moisture and lake \ninduced instability for a few isolated pop up showers to form this \nmorning across Oneida County in the west-northwest flow pattern. \nOtherwise, the rest of the area should see sunny to mostly sunny \nskies today along with slightly breezy winds, between 10-20 mph; a \nfew higher gusts to 25 mph will be possible near I-90, east to the \nMohawk Valley region. Temperatures will be seasonably warm, with \nhighs in the 60s areawide. Tonight features high pressure, \ndiminishing winds and cooler temperatures. There will be some valley \nfog developing, and frost for the colder locations (in-active \ngrowing season zones).\n\nSaturday sees the surface high sliding east toward southern New \nEngland and a high amplitude 500mb ridge moving overhead. This will \nkeep conditions dry, mainly sunny and warm, with a light south wind.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWe will see increasing clouds late Saturday night as the remnants of \nHurricane Isaias push north into the Tennessee Valley. The moisture \nwill stream well out ahead of the remnant center, reaching into west-\ncentral PA by daybreak Sunday morning. With more clouds and a steady \nsoutheast wind, it looks to stay milder in the mid-40s to low 50s \nSaturday night.\n\nThe rain is expected to quickly overspread the area from southwest \nto northeast during the day on Sunday. Model guidance has come into \nbetter agreement on this timing, with the rain reaching Scranton--\nElmira and Hornell during the mid to late morning hours. Then, \nreaching Penn Yan, Ithaca and Binghamton by early afternoon. The \nrain eventually reaches Syracuse, Rome and Cooperstown by late \nSunday afternoon. There could of course still be some fluctuations to \nthis expected timing as this is still 3 days out in time. Rainfall \namounts are forecast to be mainly between 0.50 to 1.00 inches from \nthis system, as it will be fairly fast moving. The steady rain \ntapers to showers Sunday night. Temperatures will be held down by \nthe clouds and showers, with highs in the upper 50s to mid-60s. \nGenerally light southeast winds between 6-15 mph are forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nSome uncertainty still remains, but overall the NBM ensemble \nguidance is keeping the area dry on Columbus Day, but with plenty of \nlingering clouds around. Temperatures are forecast to be warm, in \nthe mid-60s to low 70s despite the clouds. The next fast moving \nfront will try to push in from the northwest, sometime on or around \nTuesday next week. With southwest winds out ahead of the front the \nlatest guidance is showing rather warm temperatures in the mid-60s \nto low 70s once again. Early indications are that high pressure and \ndry weather return for the area Wednesday and next Thursday with \nseasonable temperatures expected.",
